---
title: "Running it"
description: "Local development, the test suite and its database service, Compose, and the deployment this repository targets."
---

## Prerequisites

Bun, Docker with Compose, and a bot token from
[@BotFather](https://t.me/BotFather) (`/newbot`, then copy the token).

## First run

```sh
cp .env.example .env
```

Fill in `BOT_TOKEN` and a `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` of your choosing. `DATABASE_URL`
already matches `docker-compose.yml`; leave it. `.env` is gitignored.

```sh
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f bot
```

You should see `@<yourbot> polling`.

::: {.callout-warning}
## Any change under `src/` needs `--build`

```sh
docker compose up -d --build     # correct
docker compose restart bot       # silently runs the old code
```

The `Dockerfile` does `COPY src ./src` and nothing bind-mounts `src`. A restart
gives you no error and no log line, just the previous behaviour.
:::

## Why `bun run start` does not work as shipped

`DATABASE_URL` in `.env` is `postgres://bot:...@db:5432/bot`, the Compose network
hostname, which does not resolve from the host. The bot sits in
`waitForDatabase`'s 30-second retry loop and then throws.

Either run it through Compose, or override the variable for the one command after
uncommenting the `db` service's loopback port mapping in `docker-compose.yml`:

```sh
DATABASE_URL=postgres://bot:<pw>@localhost:5432/bot bun run src/main.ts
```

## Tests

```sh
bun run test:db                        # starts db-test, then runs everything
bun test                               # only works if db-test is already up
bun test tests/domain                  # one directory
bun test tests/db/standings.test.ts    # one file
bun test -t "divides by the full roster"   # one test by name
bunx tsc --noEmit                      # typecheck; there is no linter
```

`bun test` alone fails on `tests/db/*` unless the `db-test` service is running.
Bun auto-loads `.env`, so `TEST_DATABASE_URL` is picked up from there.

The suite is **264 tests across 14 files**, and takes a couple of seconds.

::: {.callout-important}
## Never run the `test` Compose profile on a self-hosted production host

`db-test` binds a host port (`127.0.0.1:5433`) and uses the throwaway password
`test`. It exists only for `bun test` against a disposable, `tmpfs`-backed
database.
:::

Each test file gets its own isolated Postgres schema via `freshDatabase("<name>")`
from `tests/helpers/db.ts`, ended in `afterAll`. Give each new file a unique bare
identifier.

`tests/db/restore.test.ts` is the exception: it uses **real databases** rather than
schemas, because the backup it protects dumps per database.

## What is not tested, and how it is accepted

`registration.ts`, `checkin.ts`, `reports.ts`, `group.ts`, `reminders.ts` and the
Telegram calls inside `ticker.ts` have no automated tests **by design**.

`docs/SMOKE.md` is their only acceptance basis. It is a manual checklist, and
**no phase's smoke run has been done yet.** That remains the gate on real users.
See [Status](status.tmd).

## Deployment

The target is Tietokilta's infrastructure
([`Tietokilta/infra`](https://github.com/Tietokilta/infra)): the process as a
NixOS service on `tikpannu` beside the guild's other Telegram bots, and the
database on the shared Azure PostgreSQL flexible server.

**This has not been carried out.** The infra change has not been raised, so
nothing described below is running today.

Putting the database there is also what satisfies NFR-3, and it is why the
requirement needs no backup code in this repository: that server's databases are
enumerated, dumped and shipped off-site nightly, with 7 daily plus 4 weekly
retention, from the moment the database exists.

### What the infra change contains

| Piece | Where | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Database | a Terraform module calling `modules/service_database` | The `db_name` is what the nightly backup discovery picks up. Nothing further is needed for NFR-3 |
| Bot package | `Tietokilta/tikbots` | The flake `modules/tikbots/default.nix` imports |
| NixOS service | `tikpannu-nixos-config/modules/tikbots/` | Follow `tikbot.nix`: a sops secret for the token and a `sops.templates` env file owned by the service user |
| Secrets | `tikpannu-nixos-config/modules/secrets/` | `BOT_TOKEN` and `DATABASE_URL`. The database password is generated by `service_database` |
| `DATABASE_URL` | that env file | **Must end `?sslmode=require`.** Azure PostgreSQL requires TLS, and postgres.js reads `sslmode` straight from the URL, so no code change is needed |

`scripts/dump.sh` and `scripts/restore.sh` remain for moving a self-hosted
instance by hand. They are a convenience, not the backup.

### Adding the bot to a guild chat

A board opens `https://t.me/<bot>?startgroup=<slug>` with their guild's slug from
`src/config.ts`. That binds the chat and starts a pinned standings message that
keeps itself updated.

Pinning needs the bot to be a chat admin with pin permission. Without it the same
message still appears and still updates, just unpinned, with a line asking for
admin rights until it gets them.

## Stopping the group chat features without stopping the bot

There is no dedicated switch, and neither lever is free.

- `docker compose stop bot` stops the ticker along with everything else.
  Registration, `/log`, `/me` and `/standings` all go down too.
- `DELETE FROM chats` unbinds every affected chat immediately. **This is not a
  clean off switch.** The same row carries `pinned_message_id` and
  `last_monday_week`, so deleting it destroys both. A later rebind seeds
  `last_monday_week` to the week of the rebind, which can suppress that week's
  Monday post if it had not fired yet, and the previously pinned message is
  orphaned: still pinned, no longer tracked, so the bot can neither update nor
  unpin it.
